'''''Lemna turionifera''''' is a species of [[flowering plant]] belonging to the family [[Araceae]].<ref name="POWO">{{cite web |title=Lemna turionifera Landolt {{!}} Plants of the World Online {{!}} Kew Science |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:526224-1 |website=Plants of the World Online |access-date=5 February 2021 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Taxonomy browser (Lemna turionifera) |url=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Taxonomy/Browser/wwwtax.cgi?id=161106 |access-date=2021-02-06 |website=www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov |publisher=[[National Center for Biotechnology Information]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Plants Profile for Lemna turionifera (turion duckweed) |url=http://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile?symbol=LETU2 |access-date=2021-02-06 |website=plants.usda.gov |publisher=[[U.S. Department of Agriculture]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=ITIS Standard Report Page: Lemna turionifera |url=https://www.itis.gov/servlet/SingleRpt/SingleRpt?search_topic=TSN&search_value=503362#null |access-date=2021-02-06 |website=www.itis.gov |publisher=[[Integrated Taxonomic Information System]]}}</ref>

The species is a floating freshwater [[aquatic plant]], with one, two, three or four leaves each having a single root hanging in the water. Like its close relative ''[[Lemna minor]]'', it reproduces primarily vegetatively. Unlike ''L.&nbsp;minor'', however, the species is also capable of producing [[turion (botany)|turions]], which are starch-filled fronds that sink to the substrate and remain dormant until germinating when conditions are favorable.{{citation needed|date=February 2021}}

Its native range is Northern Europe to Japan, Northern America.<ref name="POWO"/>

